                <pb facs="0484" n="456" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v14/pg456"/>

                <head>198. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On February 6, 1967, Chairman Kosygin
                    arrived in London for a week’s visit, during which he and British Prime Minister
                        Harold Wilson sought unsuccessfully to initiate peace
                    negotiations between the United States and the Democratic Republic of North
                    Vietnam. Documentation on Sunflower, as the initiative was code-named, is
                    printed in <ref target="frus:frus1964-68v05">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations,</hi> 1964–1968, volume V</ref>.
                    Memoirs by participants in the initiative include Lyndon Johnson’s <hi rendition="#italic">The Vantage Point,</hi>
                    pages 253–255; Harold Wilson’s <hi rendition="#italic">The
                        Labour Government,</hi> 1964–1970, page 442; and Chester
                        Cooper’s <hi rendition="#italic">The Lost Crusade,</hi> pages
                    350–368.</p>
            </div>
